#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6820924-ae0c-5bd0-b374-ef7b5badcc47" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Venn Diagram Given a Description of a Sample Space"}] :question/id #uuid "6616ee7f-30ed-54ef-b578-b33bdb0101bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-13070" :question/problem "A dietician has $25$ patients, $10$ of whom are smokers, and $12$ of whom have diabetes. None of the diabetic patients are smokers. Represent the corresponding sample space using a Venn diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let us call $A$ the event \"The patient is a smoker\" and $B$ the event \"The patient has diabetes.\"\n\nSince none of the diabetic patients are smokers, we draw two non-overlapping circles to represent the events $A$ and $B$.\n\n- Since there are $10$ smokers, we write the number $10$ within the circle $A$.\n- Since there are $12$ patients with diabetes, we write the number $12$ within the circle $B$.\n\nThere are $25$ patients in total, so the number of remaining patients is\n\n$$\n25 - 10 - 12 = 3\n$$\n\nTo represent the $3$ remaining patients, we write the number $3$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B$.\n\nFinally, our Venn diagram looks as follows:\n\n![](images/86/86855ecd3f1199c9a5c6fbb66ccf08d5b8dd2913d358f52d2c499039ea6fda4d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082920 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbd8cd17-5aed-557e-b301-9828d84745c3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Using a Venn Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "ad342550-d410-529f-a5c0-5b67845cff81" :question/math-academy-id "e-13071" :question/problem "The Venn diagram shown below represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes on each region of the sample space. Given that every outcome is equally likely, find $P(A\\cup B)$.\n\n![](images/34/34ab66b54fcf6be9740be1fc89bd436ccc8d334dc8b20a5769993a93bd05b2f9.png)" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S$.\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cup B$ is represented by the area that is in either circle (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/b2/b25a0b74f7b5242f783c38c81bdb4bf8025a847f7079fc90febe510fec041b6d.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A \\cup B$ is\n$20+15+25=60$.\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n$20+15+25+40=100$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{60}{100}= 0.6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082925 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083392 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9401702-a2d9-5c53-a3de-8109a75111d7" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Given a Description of a Sample Space"}] :question/id #uuid "fcdd0c3b-36f8-5452-820a-114a8e70aa49" :question/math-academy-id "e-13212" :question/problem "A survey conducted among $100$ students of a music school found that $68$ of them play piano, $39$ of them play violin, and there are $22$ students who play both instruments. Draw a Venn diagram representing this sample space. If a student is randomly selected among the $100$ students, what is the probability that they play at least one of these instruments?" :question/worked-solution "Let us call $A$ the event \"The student plays piano\" and $B$ the event \"The student plays violin.\"\n\nNote that there are $22$ students who play both piano and violin. We draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $22$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who play *only* the piano is\n$68 - 22 = 46$,\nso we write the number $46$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B$.\n- The number of students who play *only* the violin is\n$39 - 22 = 17$,\nso we write the number $17$ in the part of the circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A$.\n\nThe total number of students who play at least one of the instruments is\n\n$$\n22+46+17 = 85\n$$\n\nand there are $100$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is\n\n$$\n100 - 85 = 15\n$$\n\nTo represent these $15$ students who play neither piano nor violin, we write the number $15$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B$.\n\nTherefore, our Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](images/cc/cc61c2a5c61467ac19d5debe2c2e53dc48f06f8d153c014f4a73e6feed514900.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to students who play at least one of the instruments. The probability that a randomly selected student is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP\\left(A\\cup B\\right)=\\dfrac{85}{100}= 0.85\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6820924-ae0c-5bd0-b374-ef7b5badcc47" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Venn Diagram Given a Description of a Sample Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95c48943-e8cf-52eb-8c3a-7075385c4728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/72bc783c78e78798c87b8eee57ede740c8df18f0f6e83e0065497eedc2511a40.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081120} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6e5fb5-b24b-5f6b-9344-3e008fe670ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/74aa1c8d177607476f6bc2f87eaf3b4020d797a47f8559744e3999820bc0aa90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081121} {:answer/id #uuid "fb13240a-7e95-58e7-be19-827e2f2083df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3da8b95c5182be1fd4c027e5e6f2ce84274286c805afdc84891bd5aecd14add9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081122} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbc6bae-4679-515d-b6ab-6d20fd308a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5c/5c2828cff69078cb82222b981b261010e2c0dc7f2f688945ded9a967402fa478.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081123} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf145f5-c06d-551a-b6e2-efe50e6ef734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/69/6938c3f414dc4fa9b110d0babae434ea8079f065d121c1710c6a7d91f10dafd9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081124}] :answer-field/id #uuid "20fa1430-8dd3-5d74-994e-23d7c1d35350" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081119}] :question/id #uuid "082ab98d-c2c3-56cd-8f7b-54c7956b64aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-42349" :question/problem "A language class has $63$ students, $43$ of them are studying English, and $25$ of them are studying Spanish. There are $10$ students studying both languages.\n\nIf $A$ represents the event that a student is studying English, and $B$ represents the event that a student is studying Spanish, which of the following gives the correct Venn diagram for the corresponding sample space?"}]}
